> The challenge of floor and ceiling effects apparently led to the invention of the amusingly-named Tobit model, which apparently is implemented (apparently more than one way, in different packages) in R.
> 
> However, it's not obvious to me why floor/ceiling effects, or the spurious interactions caused by them, should be much of an issue in logistic regression (the Tobit model is intended for linear regression). After all, the whole point of the log odds transformation in logistic regression is to remove the floor and ceiling, so the S-shaped logistic curve becomes an infinitely long straight line. If the dataset consists of nothing but 0s or nothing but 1s, or if the probability of 0 vs. 1 is (nearly) 100% correlated with the independents (e.g. X1 < 3 always implies Y = 0 and X1 >= 3 always implies Y = 1), then logistic regression won't work. But otherwise logistic regression shouldn't be affected by floors or ceilings... should it?
